  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The gap is real and well-validated: SIGMA (arXiv:2601.03385) explicitly calls for Koopman-based dynamics prediction as future work, and no existing system produces a posterior distribution over collapse timing. Novelty is primarily application-side and signal-design rather than fundamental algorithmic innovation (GP regression is standard), which some reviewers may score 3, but the niche is clean and unoccupied."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Three seeds for Table 3 is bare minimum for paired t-test significance; given high variance in small-model iterative fine-tuning outcomes, p<0.05 is not guaranteed. τ derived from a single 3-generation pilot trajectory has unquantified sampling noise. The auto-mode 7–14 day experiment window leaves only 2–9 days for paper writing against the May 25 deadline."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Well-organized and detailed; all Round 2 factual corrections (Qwen3 April 2025, NeurIPS Spotlight status, ARD → isotropic kernel) are properly applied. One correctness-level ambiguity remains in the Monte Carlo sampling step for first-passage time computation."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "EMNLP 2026 special theme 'New Missions for NLP Research' explicitly solicits work on 'synthetic data feedback loops' and 'data governance' — independently confirmed via the official CFP. A successful GPCC would give iterative training practitioners the first uncertainty-aware collapse-timing tool, which has genuine downstream value."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "CORRECTNESS: GP trajectory sampling for first-passage time probability is ambiguous and likely wrong as described. P(collapse by generation T | CRI₁:t) = P(∃T' ∈ [t+1,T]: CRI_{T'} < τ) requires drawing N=2,000 FULL GP path samples from the joint posterior over all future half-generation steps simultaneously, then checking each path for any crossing below τ. If instead the paper draws 2,000 independent marginal samples at each separate T' and checks each marginal independently, the first-passage probability is underestimated because inter-step path correlations are ignored. The current description ('Monte Carlo sampling from the GP posterior predictive distribution') is ambiguous on this point and must be made explicit, as it is a correctness issue that will be probed by quantitative ML reviewers.",
    "τ UNCERTAINTY: τ is derived from a single pilot trajectory (one observation of 'CRI value at which MMLU first dropped ≥3pt'). The uncertainty on this estimate is unquantified. If the true collapse-threshold CRI has variance across runs, τ will be misset, biasing all downstream GP predictions. The paper needs either (a) a τ sensitivity sweep reporting F1@MMLU-3pt-drop for τ ± 0.5σ_CRI, or (b) a confidence interval on τ estimated from the pilot, to demonstrate that GPCC predictions are robust to pilot τ noise. Without this, a reviewer can argue the method is 'fitted to the pilot' with no generalization guarantee.",
    "MISSING CITATION: arXiv:2505.13947 'A probabilistic perspective on model collapse' (Xu et al., 2025) provides theoretical characterization of when collapse occurs in self-consuming loops via random-walk analysis. It must be cited in Related Work and explicitly differentiated from GPCC (theoretical conditions vs. operational online algorithm). Omitting it when it directly uses 'probabilistic' and 'model collapse timing' will cause a reviewer to question whether the authors are aware of the probabilistic modeling literature on this topic.",
    "TIMELINE RISK: Auto-mode experiments requiring 7–14 days plus 2–9 days for paper writing against the May 25 ARR deadline (16 days total) is extremely tight. The submission plan must explicitly address whether paper generation (tables, figures, manuscript draft) is parallelized with later experiment stages. If auto mode experiments slip to 14 days, there is physically insufficient time for a polished ARR submission.",
    "STATISTICAL POWER RISK IN TABLE 3: n=3 seeds per condition (GPCC-triggered injection vs. fixed-schedule vs. no injection) is the bare minimum for paired t-test. Given that iterative fine-tuning outcomes show high run-to-run variance in the literature on small models, achieving p<0.05 with n=3 is genuinely uncertain. The paper should budget 4 seeds per condition if ≤10 additional H100-hours remain under the 180H cap, or acknowledge explicitly that EMNLP Main acceptance is conditional on this result while Findings is not."
  ],
  "feedback": "GPCC has addressed all Round 2 critical issues correctly and is now technically sound on the main concerns: SIGMA is properly cited and differentiated with both a dedicated paragraph and Table 4b; the ARD kernel has been replaced by isotropic Matérn-3/2 (statistically sound for 10 observations); Qwen3 dates are corrected to April 2025; NeurIPS Spotlight status is accurate; sigmoid calibration is replaced by pilot-derived τ; and cross-scale generalization is honestly scoped as preliminary evidence. Three issues need fixing before submission:\n\n1. Fix the GP trajectory sampling description explicitly. State that P(collapse by T | CRI₁:t) is computed by drawing N=2,000 joint samples from the GP posterior over the vector (CRI_{t+0.5}, CRI_{t+1}, …, CRI_T) using the full posterior covariance matrix (not N independent marginal samples), then computing the fraction of paths that cross below τ at any timestep. This is the correct first-passage time estimator and is the only version that will survive reviewer scrutiny on the probabilistic modeling claim.\n\n2. Add a τ sensitivity sweep. Run GPCC with τ_low = τ − 0.5σ_CRI and τ_high = τ + 0.5σ_CRI (where σ_CRI is the CRI standard deviation in the pilot) and report F1@MMLU-3pt-drop for all three values. If F1 is stable, the paper can claim robustness to pilot noise. If F1 varies widely, the paper must acknowledge that per-model pilot calibration is required — which is still a useful result, just more honest about scope.\n\n3. Cite arXiv:2505.13947 in Related Work. Position GPCC as the operational monitoring algorithm complementing the theoretical probabilistic perspective provided by that paper. One sentence is sufficient.\n\nFor Main-track EMNLP acceptance, Table 3 needs p<0.05 on MMLU retention improvement (GPCC-triggered vs. fixed-schedule). Budget 4 seeds per condition if compute permits — the marginal cost (~8–10 additional H100-hours) is worth the statistical insurance. For Findings-track, 3 seeds with descriptive effect size is acceptable.",
  "reason": "All Round 2 critical issues resolved; remaining concerns are a correctness-level ambiguity in GP trajectory sampling, unquantified τ pilot uncertainty, a missing probabilistic-perspective citation, and a tight 16-day execution timeline — none fatal, all fixable before submission."
}
